WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.534
The Eras Tour goes on sale.

00:00:04.534 --> 00:00:07.067
Fifty thousand fans hit one checkout in the same second.

00:00:07.067 --> 00:00:09.601
The transaction manager does everything right.

00:00:09.601 --> 00:00:12.134
Every lock honored.

00:00:12.134 --> 00:00:14.668
Every commit durable.

00:00:14.668 --> 00:00:17.201
And the site still falls over.

00:00:17.201 --> 00:00:19.735
The flow held one coarse lock for minutes at a time, and under that load it is fatal.

00:00:19.735 --> 00:00:22.268
Find the three bottlenecks.

00:00:22.268 --> 00:00:24.802
Then fix them.

00:00:24.802 --> 00:00:28.323
Here is the checkout that broke.

00:00:28.323 --> 00:00:31.844
You wait hours in a virtual queue.

00:00:31.844 --> 00:00:35.365
You pick a zone, and the instant you do, the site locks the whole zone, a thousand seats, and starts a five minute timer.

00:00:35.365 --> 00:00:38.886
Now you browse.

00:00:38.886 --> 00:00:42.407
Now you type your card.

00:00:42.407 --> 00:00:45.928
The lock is still held the whole time.

00:00:45.928 --> 00:00:49.449
One slow fan freezes a thousand seats for minutes.

00:00:49.449 --> 00:00:52.970
Three things make it worse.

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:56.491
Serving the live seat counts to thousands of people at once is its own bottleneck.

00:00:56.491 --> 00:01:00.012
The zone lock blocks every other fan who wanted a seat in it.

00:01:00.012 --> 00:01:03.533
And the lock stays held through the entire payment, with those seats in limbo, neither sold nor free.

00:01:03.533 --> 00:01:07.815
Three fixes, and not one of them is a new lock.

00:01:07.815 --> 00:01:12.097
First, shrink what you lock.

00:01:12.097 --> 00:01:16.378
Before, one pick freezes the whole zone, a thousand seats, so nine hundred ninety nine other fans are blocked.

00:01:16.378 --> 00:01:20.660
After, you lock a single seat, and nobody else waits on you.

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:24.942
Same protocol, finer grain, and a hundred times the fans browsing at once.

00:01:24.942 --> 00:01:28.110
Second, cut how long you hold the lock.

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:31.279
The slowest step in checkout is a human typing.

00:01:31.279 --> 00:01:34.447
Card number.

00:01:34.447 --> 00:01:37.615
Billing address.

00:01:37.615 --> 00:01:40.783
The CVV.

00:01:40.783 --> 00:01:43.952
Slow, and none of it needs the lock.

00:01:43.952 --> 00:01:47.120
So ask yourself, what could you collect before you ever lock a seat?

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:50.288
All of it.

00:01:50.288 --> 00:01:53.456
The card, the address, the preferences, pre-filled while you wait in the queue.

00:01:53.456 --> 00:01:56.625
When your turn comes, the locked step is only the final write.

00:01:56.625 --> 00:01:59.793
Thirty seconds instead of ten minutes, and a hundred fans become ten thousand.

00:01:59.793 --> 00:02:04.716
Third, when the load still beats the locks, stop blocking in real time.

00:02:04.716 --> 00:02:09.638
Before, fans wait hours in a queue, holding a lock the whole time.

00:02:09.638 --> 00:02:14.561
After, every request is stamped and submitted instantly.

00:02:14.561 --> 00:02:19.483
A background job works through them in order and emails the confirmation.

00:02:19.483 --> 00:02:24.406
The three hour queue becomes a sixty second submit, and about fifty thousand fans fit at once.

00:02:24.406 --> 00:02:27.668
Three moves, one lesson.

00:02:27.668 --> 00:02:30.931
Shrink how much you lock.

00:02:30.931 --> 00:02:34.193
Shorten how long you hold it, by pre-filling everything a human types before you take the lock.

00:02:34.193 --> 00:02:37.456
And when contention is unavoidable, fall back to a timestamp queue, and make the wait predictable.

00:02:37.456 --> 00:02:40.718
No new lock.

00:02:40.718 --> 00:02:43.980
No new protocol.

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:47.243
The whole win is in how the application uses the one it already has.

00:02:47.243 --> 00:02:50.505
Next, we push past the protocol itself.
